  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "R1 and R10 identify the reliability of empirical interatomic potentials at elevated temperature and their ability to reproduce DFT-derived Warren–Cowley short-range-order behavior as issues in a closely related refractory multicomponent alloy. This points toward replacing an inadequate EAM/MEAM description with a more accurate potential. R8 provides only generic support for incorporating atomic-occupancy information, while R17 shows that electronic or magnetic free-energy contributions can affect phase stability. However, none of the records proposes a machine-learning MTP, matching the DFT phase-space distribution, using FEP in place of thermodynamic integration, exploiting low MTP–DFT energy-difference variance, or calculating the DOS-gap and SRO contributions to melting. Thus the records indicate the broad need for improved potentials and physical free-energy terms, but reconstructing the specific MTP–FEP method would require substantial new ideas."
